        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Anhydrite is an anhydrous calcium sulphate, that is, gypsum without water. It forms in evaporite basins, when seas and lagoons evaporate in hot climates: calcium sulphate first precipitates as gypsum, then, once buried, loses its water through heat and becomes anhydrite; it can also crystallise directly from very concentrated brines. It is also found in hydrothermal veins and salt-dome caprocks. Brought back near the surface, it slowly rehydrates into gypsum. Its blue variety is sold as angelite.",
            "Orthorhombic system. Tabular or prismatic crystals, granular, fibrous or compact masses. Three cleavages at right angles, perfect to good, giving almost cubic fragments; uneven to splintery fracture. Hardness 3 to 3.5; specific gravity 2.90 to 3.00; vitreous to pearly lustre; refractive index 1.570 to 1.614; strong birefringence, 0.043 (biaxial positive). Colourless, white, grey, lavender blue or violetish. Soft and sensitive to water, it is rarely cut into faceted collector stones; the massive variety is polished into objects and tumbled stones.",
            "Italy: Sicily (sulphur and evaporite deposits) and the Alps (Simplon Pass). Mexico: Naica (Chihuahua), remarkable crystals. Germany (Stassfurt, Bad Sulza), Poland (Wieliczka), Switzerland (Bex), Austria, France (Lorraine, in the salt basins), Peru (angelite), Canada and the United States (New Mexico, Texas). It forms enormous layers in sedimentary basins worldwide.",
            "A stone of acceptance, patience and grounding, said to help let go of the past and accept situations. It is associated with the throat and crown chakras and the sign of Cancer. Never put it in water; cleanse by smudging or sound; recharge in moonlight."
