        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Baryte, or barite, is a barium sulphate, a remarkably heavy mineral. It forms in low- to moderate-temperature hydrothermal veins, often with fluorite, calcite, galena and sphalerite, when barium-bearing fluids meet sulphate-rich waters. It also occurs in marine sediments, as nodules and concretions, and in desert sands where it forms baryte “desert roses”. It can be colourless, white, yellow, blue, brown or golden, depending on impurities and defects caused by natural radioactivity.",
            "Orthorhombic system. Thick tabular crystals, in crests or rosettes; lamellar masses. Perfect basal and good prismatic cleavage: very fragile; uneven fracture. Hardness 3 to 3.5; specific gravity 4.3 to 4.6, surprisingly heavy for a pale mineral, hence its Greek name “barys”, heavy; vitreous to pearly lustre; refractive index 1.636 to 1.648; birefringence 0.012. Variable fluorescence. Too soft and cleavable for jewellery, it is rarely cut into faceted collector stones, often blue or golden. It is used in drilling muds and as a contrast agent in medical imaging.",
